\f0\fs24 \cf0 Dear All,\
\
Another Page from Fontiveros:\
Let's meet Julian\
\
\
At first glance, Julian appears to be seventy, perhaps even younger. But beneath that spirited smile is the history of nearly a century. He is, without a doubt, my favorite encounter in Fontiveros.\
\
He had attended my performance last year, and he appeared again for this opening of the exhibition. He stood before the paintings, visibly moved, yet silent\'97but smiling. \
\
It wasn\'92t until the second day, during the procession at the church, that our paths truly crossed.\
The moment I entered the church, our story really began. He smiled and asked me to come forward.\
What followed there is a story for my final page; it was the best part of all. (Tomorrow)\
\
Once the procession ended, with Jos\'e9 and Jaime acting as my linguistic bridges, Julian\'92s excitement bubbled over. He led me through the church with a youthful vigor, pointing out the font where St. John of the Cross was baptized and the hallowed ground where the Saint\'92s father is buried. When I asked if he, too, was baptized in that same font, his face lit up as he said, "Yes."\
\
He insisted we join him at the local bar for a drink. His joy was infectious, his smile a permanent fixture as he shared fragments of his life. For years, he ran a shop selling cigarettes and provisions\'97a business over 150 years old, inherited from his grandfather. Today, his nephew runs the shop while Julian tends to his orchard.\
\
Suddenly, he hopped up and crossed the road, as his house was opposite. He returned clutching a treasure: \
I'm here for the tricentenary, 300 years later the canonisation of John as Saint.\
\
He shows me a 100-year-old poster printed for the second centenary of St. John\'92s canonization.\
 He handed it to me\'97as a gift. I was speechless.\
\
The House Like a Wedding Cake\
If the gift wasn't enough, Julian invited us into his home. It felt like stepping into a wedding cake or a Wes Anderson film\'97layers upon layers of history. Inside was a treasure box of pink basins and vintage wallpapers. He lives alone, and his sister lives next door . Both never married.\
 in this massive, six-bedroom ancestral home, surrounded by the echoes of three generations. At ninety years old, he moves through it with a grace that defies time.\
\
The Singing of the Cloister\
On Day 3, I gifted him some Indian incense. He came to the exhibition asking me to come see the blessed sacrament to the chapel of the cloistered nuns. It may surprise many that Spain still maintains these silent sanctuaries\'97convents where women devote their entire lives to prayer, never leaving or maintaining contact with the outside world. - THE CLOISTERS \
\
We sat in the chapel where the Blessed Sacrament was exposed. Just as we prepared to leave after five minutes, the nuns began their Vespers from behind the grilled gates. Their hymns drifted out like angels singing from the clouds. We stayed for twenty minutes, anchored by the celestial sound.\
\
As we walked away, Julian turned to Jos\'e9 and whispered, "Omkar is very devotional." Julian\'97the ninety-year-old man who never stops smiling.\
\
But as I said, the best part of this story is yet to come.\
\
Warmly,\
Omkar}